    {# ============ WHAT IT IS ============ #}
    <section class="section fade-up fade-up-4">
        <h2>What a vault is</h2>

        <p>Your vault is a <strong>memory graph</strong>: every note you drop in, every tag, every <code>[[wikilink]]</code> between them, in one store your AI can read and write directly. It's a knowledge graph behind a plain HTTP API and <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP</a> &mdash; the open standard AI assistants speak &mdash; so connecting is one URL, not an integration project.</p>

        <p>Jot &ldquo;call the dentist&rdquo; one day and &ldquo;Maya's birthday is in June&rdquo; the next. The vault holds both, and the links between them, so the next thing you ask your AI lands <em>in context</em> instead of on a blank page. You capture; the graph connects; your AI remembers.</p>
    </section>

    {# ============ WHY IT'S THE FOUNDATION ============ #}
    <section class="section fade-up fade-up-4">
        <h2>Why it's the foundation</h2>

        <p>Everything else in Parachute is built on the vault. Get this one thing and the rest of the ladder is just ways to reach it.</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Capture anything, it connects.</strong> Notes, tags, links, voice memos turned to text &mdash; one graph, full-text searchable, that grows more useful the more you put in it.</li>
            <li><strong>Any AI can read it.</strong> Point Claude, ChatGPT, Claude Code, Cursor, or your own agent at one MCP URL. They all read and write the same source of truth &mdash; no more re-explaining yourself to a fresh chat.</li>
            <li><strong>It stays yours.</strong> The vault lives on a box you control, in the open. Nothing round-trips through us. Export the whole thing to portable, git-trackable markdown with one command &mdash; leaving is never a migration project.</li>
            <li><strong>It outlives the tool.</strong> Chats end, apps come and go. Your memory shouldn't. The vault is the layer that stays put underneath all of them.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    {# ============ WHEN YOU WANT ONE ============ #}
    <section class="section fade-up fade-up-4">
        <h2>When you want one</h2>

        <p>The moment you notice you're <em>re-explaining yourself</em> to your AI &mdash; the same context, the same background, again &mdash; that's the moment for a vault. The same goes for the thought you had last week that's already gone, or the notes scattered across three apps that never talk to each other. A vault is where all of that lands and stays connected.</p>

        <p class="muted">Not sure yet? That's fine. A vault is worth having the instant your thinking is worth keeping &mdash; you don't need a plan for it first. Start empty and let it fill.</p>
    </section>

    {# ============ SET UP YOUR VAULT (matches the hub's vault-setup on-ramp) ============ #}
    <section class="section fade-up fade-up-5">
        <h2>Set up your vault</h2>

        <p>Once your vault is connected to your AI, you don't have to design it alone. Paste this prompt and let your AI interview you &mdash; it asks where your notes live now and proposes a tag-and-path structure that fits how <em>you</em> actually think.</p>

        <div class="code-inline" style="text-align: left;">
<code>I just set up a Parachute vault &mdash; a memory graph you can read and write<br>
over MCP. Before I start filling it, interview me:<br>
<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&bull; Ask where my notes and thinking live right now (apps, docs, my head).<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&bull; Ask what you should remember about me and the work I do.<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&bull; Then propose a tag + folder structure that fits how I think, and<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;create a few starter notes so the graph isn't empty.<br>
<br>
Keep it a conversation &mdash; one or two questions at a time.</code>
        </div>

        <p class="muted">New here? A fresh vault opens with a <strong>Getting Started</strong> note your AI can read to help shape it. Connecting your AI is one MCP URL &mdash; the <a href="/start/">Get started</a> guide walks it.</p>
    </section>

